WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.080
How do resource graphs reveal

2
00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.200
deadlock? A deadlock is a

3
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.880
set of blocked processes whose

4
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.060
required resources or events can

5
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.600
only be supplied by members

6
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.100
of that same blocked set.

7
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.900
Draw the ownership and requests

8
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.880
separately before deciding what can

9
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:15.220
progress.

10
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.380
A resource type may have

11
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.940
several instances. A process requests

12
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.000
an instance, uses it after

13
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.500
allocation, and eventually releases it.

14
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.120
A resource-allocation graph records those

15
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.500
relationships rather than merely drawing

16
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.980
processes that happen to be

17
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:31.340
slow.

18
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.620
A request edge points from

19
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:34.960
a process to a resource

20
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.040
type. An assignment edge points

21
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.680
from a resource instance to

22
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.380
the owning process. Reversing either

23
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.280
arrow changes the meaning, so

24
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.920
label the relationship as you

25
00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:45.520
draw it.

26
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.260
Consider two single-instance locks, A

27
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.920
and B. P zero owns

28
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.620
A, while P one owns

29
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.760
B. So far neither process

30
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.460
is necessarily blocked; ownership alone

31
00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:58.480
is not a deadlock.

32
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.540
Now P zero requests B

33
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.080
and blocks while retaining A.

34
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.160
P one requests A and

35
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.400
blocks while retaining B. Follow

36
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.320
the cycle: P zero needs

37
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.940
B, held by P one,

38
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.600
which needs A, held by

39
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:13.260
P zero.

40
00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.540
If neither process can release

41
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.180
its first lock until its

42
00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.780
second request succeeds, the cycle

43
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.680
cannot resolve within this model.

44
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.900
Every participant waits for another

45
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.560
participant. A scheduler cannot fix

46
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.080
it simply by giving these

47
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.780
blocked processes more turns.

48
00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.040
With one instance of every

49
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.140
resource type, a cycle implies

50
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.020
deadlock. With multiple instances, a

51
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.100
cycle indicates a possibility rather

52
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.560
than a sufficient conclusion. An

53
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.300
extra available instance or a

54
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.000
process outside the cycle that

55
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.820
can finish may still break

56
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:47.860
the wait.

57
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.300
No cycle means no deadlock

58
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.060
in this resource-allocation model. Do

59
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.060
not apply the single-instance conclusion

60
00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.420
to a drawing with several

61
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.560
dots per resource. Count the

62
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.580
actual instances and examine which

63
00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:01.620
holders can complete.

64
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.640
Starvation differs because other processes

65
00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.680
may continue completing work while

66
00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.140
one unfortunate contender is continually

67
00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.500
denied service. A deadlocked set

68
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.120
cannot make the required internal

69
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.040
progress. The two problems can

70
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.540
share symptoms without sharing the

71
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:17.040
same proof.

72
00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:18.680
Things to take note of.

73
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.020
Draw request arrows from process

74
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:22.820
to resource and assignment arrows

75
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.520
back to the owner. Use

76
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.180
the instance count when interpreting

77
00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.940
a cycle, and describe the

78
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.320
blocked dependency rather than guessing

79
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:31.620
from an idle processor.
